  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/df/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_20.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed discovers something inside a meteorite that landed on a farmer's land and indicates that \"this <i>proves</i> that some form of life <i>must</i> exist in outer space!\"&nbsp; But before Reed can study it any more, Johnny sees a \"huge globe\" floating over the crowds down in the street below.\n\nWhen they investigate, they are sucked into it and find themselves face-to-face with the Watcher (who initially appeared in [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13]).&nbsp; His race is forbidden from interfering, but a menace to the entire universe has appeared, and he knows the FF could possibly help.&nbsp; He shows them the accident at ACME Atomics Corp. that turned a hapless lab technician into the Molecule Man!\n\nWhen they return, the Baxter Building is gone.&nbsp; But they soon see it hovering in the air above Times Square!&nbsp; The Molecule Man now declares he's in total control of the world!&nbsp; He's brandishing a \"molecular wand\" (<i>and still never told where it came from</i>).\n\nA couple of hazards follow, but then he creates two magnets that adhere to Mr. Fantastic's flexible body.&nbsp; He points out that one is \"positive\" and one is \"negative\" which repel one another, stretching Mr. Fantastic's body as they fly in opposite directions.&nbsp; The Human Torch is forced to melt the magnets before Reed is stretched beyond his limits.\n\nSeveral pages of attemps to tackle the Molecule Man (including invisible Sue trying to wrest his wand away from him, to no avail) ultimately drive them to flee the scene to think of a plan to defeat him.&nbsp; Naturally the watching crowd brand them \"quitters.\"&nbsp; The Molecule Man (MM) orders the FF be found, so now they're being hunted by everyone!&nbsp; But they're rescued by one of the Yancy Street Gang and secretly hide in Alicia's apartment.\n\nReed then realizes that MM can only affect <i>inorganic</i> molecules -- nothing actually <i>alive</i> -- and this gives him an idea.&nbsp; Alicia coats them with plaster and then summons MM, who attempts to rearrange the molecules of what he thinks are statues, but the attempt backfires, and the feedback is \"too painful for him to endure!\"\n\nAfter MM is thrown backward, Reed grabs his wand, and at that moment, the huge glowing sphere of the Watcher appears and absorbs the Molecule Man.&nbsp; The Watcher was seeing everything, and stepped in to take MM after they had defeated him.&nbsp; He restores the Baxter Building to its proper location and will \"take the Molecule Man away...he will never menace you again!\"\n\nThe world is now safe again, and Reed wonders what to do with \"this now-harmless little wand!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "Reed starts off the issue examining a meteorite that landed on a farmer's land, and once they find its organic core, he says \"this <i>proves</i> that some form of life <i>must</i> exist in outer space!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Good grief, Reed!]\n\nYou'd think that the FF's encounter with the Skrulls back in [LINK:1961:FF_2|FF #2], not to <i>mention</i> the Super-Skrull from a couple of issues ago ([LINK:1963:FF_18|FF #18]), the trip the team took out to Planet X in [LINK:1962:FF_7|FF #7], the story that Dr. Doom told you about where <i>he</i> was before his return in [LINK:1962:FF_10|FF #10], your encounter with the Impossible Man back in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11ff], and the ruins you found on the Moon in [LINK:1963:FF_13|FF #13] -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=you'd <i>think</i> that would be <i>more</i> than enough evidence!]\n\nBut soon, all of that is moot, as the Watcher appears and indicates on page 5 that a \"small, puny, ridiculous-looking\" lab tech suddenly has gained the \"power to destroy worlds, galaxies, yes... even <i>universes</i>!\"\n[ASSET: center | RidiculousLooking | width=67%]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(Hey Watcher!&nbsp; You call that guy \"ridiculous-looking?\"&nbsp; Look at what <b>you're</b> wearing, dude!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe Watcher rationalizes his overt warning to the Fantastic Four as being necessary because the threat the new Molecule Man poses could destroy even his <i>own</i> race.&nbsp; And when he recounts the tale of the hapless lab tech that got in an atomic accident, he forgets one <i>not-so-minor</i> detail.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Where did the Molecule Man's <i>wand</i> come from?]\n\nThe accident that affects the hapless worker, as the Watcher's flashback tale portrays, and his face is transformed, leaving us with the impression that he <i>also</i> has been transformed to have this mind-boggling power over molecules, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=but the wand seems to come out of nowhere] -- nothing is said of its origin, so it's a complete mystery.\n\n[ASSET: center | MysteriousWand | width=67%]\n\nAn accident that transforms a man and somehow mystically forms a wand, without which he's <i>powerless</i>?&nbsp; This seems hard to believe, particularly since Jack Kirby did such a <i>fantastic job</i> with MM's appearance.&nbsp; Not only do we see the lightning bolt lines emanating out of the middle of his face, the jagged mouth (making him look even <i>more</i> sinister), was so well done, I awarded an extra half point for that alone!\n\nWhat a <i>great</i> looking villain!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Superb job there, Jack!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nOn a side note, [SPAN: severity=success; text=kudos to Stan] for using the teenager in the group to ask the question, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Reed, what exactly <i>are</i> molecules?\"]&nbsp; Remember, we were told <b><i>only last month</i></b> in [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] that Johnny Storm is 16 years old and \"senior in high school,\" so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=you'd <i>expect</i> him to already know what a molecule is.]\n\nThat being said, I still understand (and even <i>appreciate</i>) the storytelling device Stan used to clue in all his readers.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | HoveringBaxterBuilding | width=21%] The Watcher indicates at the top of page 7 that he's \"powerless to act -- my function is only to <i>watch</i>!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=I beg to differ!]&nbsp; You <i>just finished</i> drawing the Fantastic Four into \"some other dimension\" (according to Reed on page 4) and <i>warned</i> them about the new threat!\n[BR]\n[BR] Not only that, by removing them from New York City, you spared them from being lifted into the air with the rest of the Baxter Building!\n[BR]\n[BR] So, I'm <i>sorry</i>, Watcher, but -- [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=You lie!]\n\n(And while I'm on the subject of the Baxter Building, this is the <i>second time</i> it has been pulled up from its foundations!&nbsp; Does anyone remember [LINK:1962:FF_6|FF #6] and its physics-optional journey up into space?)\n\nNaturally, the power has gone to the Molecule Man's head, and he <i>demands</i> \"my every command is to be obeyed instantly -- without hesitation -- without question!\"&nbsp; And when Reed objects, he conjures up a couple of magnets, pointing out that one is \"positive\" and other \"negative\" which repel one another, stretching Mr. Fantastic's body as they fly in opposite directions.\n[ASSET: center | Magnets | width=67%]\n\nI'm sure I'm not the only reader who wonders:\n[HANG] How are these magnets attracted to human flesh instead of metal?\n[HANG] Don't magnets come, each with <i>both</i> positive and negative sides?\n[HANG] And even if they don't, don't positive and negative <i>attract</i>, rather than <i>repel</i>?\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=Stan may know what a molecule is, but he and Jack must have missed the lecture on magnets!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | Quitters | width=12%] [ASSET: right | HuntThemDown | width=12%] Pages 9-15 form the bulk of the issue's seven pages of fruitless battle against the Molecule Man, culminating in the FF falling back to \"formulate some new strategy!\"&nbsp; <i>Naturally</i>, the innocent bystanders are quick to label them \"quitters!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] The Molecule Man knows they have no headquarters to return to, so he orders the citizens to [SPAN: severity=warning; text=hunt them down!]\n\nIronically, the Yancy Street Gang comes to their rescue and provides a place for them to hide, indicating that they want the FF, specifically the <i>Thing</i>, \"all to ourselves!\"&nbsp; And the Thing's response?&nbsp; \"This is <i>humiliatin'!</i>  Being rescued by a Yancy Streeter!\"&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nThe Fantastic Four reduced to accepting help from the Yancy Street Gang was absolutely a laugh-worthy moment -- [SPAN: severity=success; text=Great plot beat, Stan and Jack!]\n\n[ASSET: right | OrganicMolecules | width=12%] The issue ends with Reed's deduction that the Molecule Man can only affect <i>inorganic</i> molecules, so once Alicia coated the members of the Fantastic Four to make them look like statues, and the villain attempted to affect the stone, he gets caught in a feedback loop and is then drawn away by the Watcher before he can grab his \"power wand,\" which Reed stretches out and knocks away from his reach.\n\nThe Watcher ensures them that the Molecule Man will \"never menace you again,\" restores New York City to normal (including their hijacked headquarters), and the [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day is saved.]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nA villain like the Molecule Man that poses enough of a threat to make even a being as powerful as the Watcher take note (though one would expect him to be aware of <i>most</i> things on Earth) -- that's a menace that makes one sit up straighter and take notice.\n\nObviously, with a power like the Molecule Man, it must have been somewhat fun for Kirby to have dreamed up one threat after another for the FF to battle -- conjured up out of nearby molecules -- and letting Stan fill in the dialog.&nbsp; This seems the perfect type of issue for that sort of working arrangement, kind of like what the Impossible Man was back in [LINK:1962:FF_11_ff1|FF #11], not to mention the X-Men's training room, where Kirby's imagination is free to run wild, as long as he leaves enough pages to wrap things up!\n\nAnd on the one hand, you have to hand it to Jack -- he consumed a good chunk of the book just in the initial battle that ultimately drove the FF to retreat.&nbsp; Finally with time to think, Reed came up with a solution, and with the help of Alicia, the threat was turned away, and everything came out fine.\n\nThat's all well and good -- the book was <b>never dull</b>, in fact.\n\nBut it's still fair to wonder how the Watcher can justify his warning to the FF at the beginning of the story, not to <i>mention</i> the fact that he obviously waited around for Reed to solve the puzzle, so that he could ultimately swoop down and whisk the Molecule Man away, leaving only his power wand.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=And there there's <i>that</i> -- the power wand itself...]\n\nAs I mentioned above, the wand magically appears on page 6 after the atomic explosion that gives the \"ridiculous-looking\" lab technician his powers.&nbsp; An explosion powerful enough to make a mess out of the Molecule Man's face would not be expected to mystically add a perfectly-shaped magic wand appear to boot!\n\nIt makes the reader wonder if the wand wasn't simply a creation of the Molecule Man himself, as an outward symbol of his power (i.e. it isn't essential to him being able to manipulate molecules).&nbsp; However, his words to Reed in the middle of page 21, where he says, \"I can still reach my power want before anything can stop me!\" seems to suggest that it <i>is</i>, in fact, necessary.\n\nBut Reed's comment in the issue final panel about the wand being \"now-harmless\" -- that implies that it is useless <i>without</i> the Molecule Man being present.&nbsp; One is forced to conclude then that it derives its power from the Molecule Man himself.\n\nBecause of this confusion (and I really <i>do</i> walk away scratching my head), it's difficult to conclude that the story is anywhere close to excellent.&nbsp; It had several good plot beats, namely the reappearance of the fascinating Watcher, the fact that Alicia played a role in saving the day, and of course, the Yancy Street Gang rescue (which was, by far, the best -- <i>and</i> most unexpected of all).\n\nBut the Watcher's behavior -- mysteriously inconsistent to say the <i>least</i>, interfering, but <i>not really</i> -- together with the mysterious power wand that, in one moment seems necessary, and the next, redundant... both of these argue for a reduction in grade.\n\nIn the end, the story deserves to be commended for its ambition -- a villain powerful enough for the Watcher to take notice -- <i>that's </i>no laughing matter!&nbsp; But, conversely, was the Watcher even necessary in this tale, other than to wave his hands at the end and whisk the villain away?&nbsp; That's debatable.\n\nThrow the power wand head-scratcher into the mix as well, and you still have a book worth reading, but one that is only solid enough to be mildly enjoyed.",
  "side_note": [
    "Why does Johnny constantly stand around in FF headquarters always flamed on?&nbsp; Wouldn't he rather conserve his limited flame to when he needs it?&nbsp; <i>Yeah okay</i>, it's more visually appealing this way, especially for the splash page -- I get that, but <i>still</i>....\n\nAt least he had the sense to flame off when they're at Alicia's apartment!"
  ],
  "letters_page": "None other than future Marvel writer/editor Mark Gruenwald (then only 10 years old) writes in that his FF fan club (which has him as secretary) loves the pin-up pages \"because they're perfect for decorating our clubhouse.\"&nbsp; <i>What</i>?&nbsp; <i>Oh Mark -- you <b>didn't</b> cut up all those wonderful old (not to mention <b>valuable</b>) FF issues, did you?&nbsp; </i>[SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Argh!]\n\n<i>Heh</i>, Don Keefer writes:&nbsp; \"Dear Stan and Jack, How come we are supposed to write to 'Dear Stan and Jack' instead of 'Dear Jack and Stan'???\"&nbsp; with the response being:&nbsp; <i>Because it's Stan's typewriter and so HE makes up the rules -- you troublemaker!</i>\n\nWhen Kevin Shields says \"...I only bought #13 for the sake of something to read.\" the editor replies with:&nbsp; <i>You only bought it for something to READ?&nbsp; What do you think the REST of us buy them for -- paper dolls?</i>  [SPAN: severity=success; text=(<i>Heh</i> - well done!)]\n\nThe letters page also includes one from George R. Martin - the creator of the <i>A Song of Ice and Fire</i> series that led to <i>Game of Thrones</i>.",
  "ownership": "As for me, I got two just under mid-grade copies as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid $27.50 and $36.85 for.&nbsp; They are prepped and listed on eBay for sale, so we'll see what happens....",
  "decoder_ring": "The Molecule Man is a significant threat, as we see numerous times over the decades ahead, but this was the first time I'd actually sat down to read his initial appearance.&nbsp; My own first exposure to him was in FF #187 -- still with a wand, but a <i>much</i> cooler looking one!",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}